PREAMBLE

The Polyglot FFI Contract Verifier (“PFCV”) is a high-integrity computational architecture developed to analyze, verify, and enforce correctness across foreign function interfaces and polyglot runtime boundaries. The Software embodies structured constraint evaluation models, execution graph validation logic, ownership registry concepts, and cross-language verification methodologies designed to preserve memory safety and interface integrity.

The Antigravity Source-Available and Technical Protection License (“ASTPL”) is established to enable public transparency, technical auditability, and academic experimentation while preventing unauthorized commercial exploitation, service-based wrapping, artificial intelligence ingestion, or derivative monetization of the Software and its underlying methodologies.

This License is not an open-source license. It is a restrictive source-available agreement. Accessing, downloading, cloning, copying, modifying, or otherwise using the Software constitutes acceptance of this License as a binding legal agreement.

1. DEFINITIONS

1.1 “Software” means the PFCV repository in its entirety, including source code, object code, documentation, architectural descriptions, constraint schemas, execution graphs, contract definitions, testing frameworks, prompts, configuration files, and related materials.

1.2 “Protected Technical Methodologies” means the computational logic, algebraic constraint systems, recursive structural alignment techniques, ownership modeling constructs, validation strategies, runtime enforcement mechanisms, and architectural interaction patterns embodied in the Software.

1.3 “Commercial Use” means any use that directly or indirectly generates revenue, commercial advantage, strategic benefit, operational efficiency, or business value for a for-profit entity. Commercial Use includes, without limitation:
(a) sale, licensing, or monetization of the Software;
(b) embedding the Software within a commercial product;
(c) integration into paid services or subscription platforms;
(d) internal operational deployment within a for-profit organization;
(e) use by venture-backed or investor-funded entities;
(f) integration into compliance, infrastructure, DevOps, or production systems;
(g) use that contributes to cost reduction, risk mitigation, or business advantage in a commercial context.

1.4 “Non-Commercial Use” means personal experimentation, academic research, scholarly analysis, and educational use that does not generate revenue or confer commercial competitive advantage.

1.5 “Network Access Provision” means making the functionality of the Software, or derivative functionality based upon it, available through APIs, SaaS platforms, hosted environments, remote execution systems, or any network-accessible interface.

1.6 “Machine Training Use” means ingestion, scraping, automated extraction, dataset compilation, fine-tuning, model weight adjustment, model distillation, pattern extraction, or any activity intended to train, enhance, or improve artificial intelligence or machine learning systems.

